   "source": "## Data\n\n### Data Overview\n\nWe use two complementary datasets that together cover the variables needed to address our research question. Both datasets contain information on production budget, genre, country of production, and financial/critical success metrics. Dataset 1 provides IMDb scores and gross revenue with a focus on the 1986\u20132016 period, while Dataset 2 extends coverage through 2017 and adds variables such as original language, cast, and popularity score. Together they allow us to examine how budget, genre, language, talent, and geography jointly relate to a film's financial and critical success, and how these relationships differ across the pre- and post-2015 streaming eras.\n\n**Combining the datasets:** We merge the two datasets on movie title and release year after standardizing column names. Since Dataset 1 contains IMDb score and gross revenue and Dataset 2 contains popularity and additional cast/language detail, the merge produces a richer combined dataset."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### Dataset #1 \u2014 Movie Industry\n\nThis dataset contains 6,820 films released between 1986 and 2016 with 15 variables covering production details, financial performance, and audience reception. The core financial variables are `budget` (production cost in USD) and `gross` (total theatrical box office revenue in USD). The `score` variable is an IMDb user rating on a 0\u201310 scale and serves as our primary measure of critical/audience success.\n\n- **Link:** https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/danielgrijalvas/movies/data\n- **Observations:** 6,820 | **Variables:** 15\n- **Key variables:** `budget`, `gross`, `score`, `genre`, `star`, `country`, `year`, `rating`, `runtime`\n- **Shortcomings:** Missing/zero budget values for many films; coverage ends at 2016; single genre per film; IMDb scores may be biased toward popular Western films."
